winewayland: reuse the subsurface z-order walk while the Win32 stacking is unchanged

Lightroom Classic: "significant lag when I click on masking". The first Masking open of a session builds ~90
windows. Measured on a nested KWin (his desk layout, 175%), clean-room LrC 15.6, runtime 124: Shift+W to the last
window event 13.5 s / 14.5 s; neutron-stack-stats "calls=4023 applies=0" per 5 s: the subsurface restack ran ~800
times a second and changed the stacking zero times. perf on the UI thread: 48% in neutron_zorder_collect, the
phase-A walk that runs before every restack. Per visited window it made a GetProp by name, a monitor-dpi lookup +
GetWindowRect and two GetWindowRelative calls, each a wineserver round trip.

- A process-wide generation counter (neutron_zorder_generation) is bumped wherever the order the mirror follows
  can change: a SetWindowPos without SWP_NOZORDER, a show or hide, DestroyWindow, SetWindowStyle, a subsurface
  role set or cleared, a client wl_subsurface (re)created. A plain move or resize does not bump it.
- neutron_zorder_get() reuses the last walk for that toplevel while the generation is unchanged (4 entries). The
  generation is sampled before the walk, so a change during the walk leaves the entry stale and the next call
  walks again. The CEF-host flag is re-read on every reuse (dcomp sets it without any window change).
- The walk reads a window's rect only when the opt-in temporal rule (its one reader) is on, and the CEF-host
  property only for a window that has a client surface (the only ones phase B reads it for).
Phase B, the restack itself, is unchanged and still runs on every update.

Measured, same binary, fresh launch each: reuse 5.1 s / 4.5 s; NEUTRON_ZORDER_REUSE=0 14.5 s / 13.5 s. The
Masking panel and its flyout draw normally above the GPU canvas.

Kill switch NEUTRON_ZORDER_REUSE=0 restores the full walk on every update.

--- a/dlls/winewayland.drv/waylanddrv.h
+++ b/dlls/winewayland.drv/waylanddrv.h
